\cfg{man-identity}{cputty}{1}{2026-11-07}{PuTTY tool suite}{PuTTY tool suite}

\H{cputty-manpage} Man page for cputty

\S{cputty-manpage-name} NAME

\cw{cputty} \- cluster SSH client for X: one console typing into many PuTTY sessions

\S{cputty-manpage-synopsis} SYNOPSIS

\c cputty [ options ] host [ host ... ]
\e bbbbbb   iiiiiii     iiii   iiii

\S{cputty-manpage-description} DESCRIPTION

\cw{cputty} is a variant of \cw{putty}(\e{1}) in the style of the
classic \cw{cssh} cluster administration tools. It opens one PuTTY
terminal window for each \e{host} given on the command line, together
with a small \e{console} window.

Keystrokes typed into the console window are sent to every session
whose box is ticked in the console's host list. The \q{All}, \q{None}
and \q{Invert} buttons change which sessions are ticked. Keystrokes
typed into an individual terminal window go to that session only, so
you can switch freely between typing to the whole cluster and
attending to a single machine.

Each keystroke from the console is translated by each session
separately, so terminal modes such as application cursor keys are
honoured per session. Sessions whose connection has closed no longer
receive input from the console; close their windows directly.

Closing the console window closes all the sessions and exits
\cw{cputty}. \cw{cputty} also exits when the last session window is
closed.

Sessions started from a terminal window's menu (\q{New Session},
\q{Duplicate Session} and \q{Saved Sessions}) open in the same
process and join the console's host list.

\S{cputty-manpage-options} OPTIONS

\cw{cputty} accepts the same options as \cw{putty}(\e{1}). Each
option applies to every host. Each \e{host} argument is interpreted as
\cw{putty} would interpret its own host argument, so for example
\cw{user@host} is accepted.

Unlike \cw{putty}, \cw{cputty} treats \e{every} non-option argument as
a host name. To connect to a non-default port, use \cw{\-P}
\e{port}.

If no \e{host} is given, \cw{cputty} starts a single session as
\cw{putty} would (from \cw{\-load}, or via the configuration box).
More sessions can then be added from the terminal window's menu.

\S{cputty-manpage-examples} EXAMPLES

\c cputty -l root web1 web2 web3

\c cputty -ssh -i ~/.ssh/admin.ppk admin@db1 admin@db2

\S{cputty-manpage-limitations} LIMITATIONS

A \cw{\-geometry} option applies to every window, so giving a window
position will place all the windows on top of each other.

A password given with \cw{\-pw} is offered to every host.

\S{cputty-manpage-see-also} SEE ALSO

\cw{putty}(\e{1})
